<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">111</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Book of the Former Han, Hanshu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Han is a Chinese history of the Western, or Former, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> from the first emperor in 206 BCE to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> in 23 CE, and one of the Twenty-Four Histories. Begun by Ban Biao as a continuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> and recast by his son Ban Gu, it was finished in 111 CE by Ban Gu's sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-zhao">Ban Zhao</a>, 19 years after he died in prison. In 100 fascicles of annals, tables, treatises and biographies, it was the first history in the annals-biography form to cover a single dynasty and set the model for the official histories until modern times. Its treatise on geography holds the first unambiguous mention of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-han.jpg" alt="Book of Han" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 松照庵 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:前漢書宋福唐郡庠刻宋元遞修本.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 2nd century BCE the settlements had become city-states; the <b>Book of Han</b> lists 36 statelets in the basin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Book of Han</b>, Han envoys could enter the Xiongnu ruler’s tents only after setting aside their tallies of office and having their faces tattooed; the envoy Wang Wu agreed and was received with honour.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Hanshu</b> took Li Kui as the first author of works on regulations and an influence on Shang Yang, under whose reforms in the 4th century BCE Legalist ideas were first adopted by a state, Qin.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Ban Gu, writing in the <b>Book of Han</b>, the work began as records kept by Confucius's disciples of his conversations with them, collected and jointly edited after his death in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/analects">Analects · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
